---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFancyText from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeFancyText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="cursed-text-generator">
  <DevEncodeFancyText
    client:load
    sample="cursed"
    featured={['cursed-light', 'cursed-medium', 'cursed-heavy']}
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What makes text look cursed</h2>
    <p>
      Unicode allows any number of combining diacritical marks on one base character. This is
      deliberate: scripts with stacked vowel and tone marks need it. Layout engines draw all marks they
      get, so thirty stacked marks climb out of the line.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cursed text, also called zalgo after the internet horror meme it came from around 2009, is only
      that: your letters with randomly chosen marks stacked above and below.
    </p>

    <h2>Three intensities</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Level</th><th>Marks per character</th><th>Behaviour</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Light</td><td>1 above, 1 below</td><td>Stays inside its own line; usually tolerated anywhere</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Medium</td><td>3 above, 3 below</td><td>Touches the lines above and below</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Heavy</td><td>6 above, 6 below</td><td>Goes far beyond the line box; the classic look</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The output is deterministic: the marks are chosen from the position of each character, not from a
      random number generator, so the same input always gives exactly the same cursed text, today or
      tomorrow.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it overflows the line</h2>
    <p>
      Text layout reserves space for a line from the declared ascent and descent of the font. Combining
      marks are drawn relative to the base character and can extend any distance, and no engine makes
      the line box larger to fit them. That overlap is the effect. It is also why moderators dislike it:
      one heavy message can make several lines of a chat log unreadable.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it will get you in trouble</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Discord: many servers have automod rules against zalgo, and heavy stacking can measurably slow
        the rendering of the client.
      </li>
      <li>
        Usernames: most platforms remove or reject combining marks in handles, and some treat the
        attempt as an impersonation risk.
      </li>
      <li>
        Anywhere it covers other people's text. Most communities enforce this rule: decorating your own
        message is fine, making the channel unreadable is not.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Light intensity is the safe default if you want the effect without problems.</p>

    <h2>It is unreadable to assistive technology</h2>
    <p>
      Screen readers handle stacked combining marks unpredictably: some read each mark by name, which
      gives a minute of speech for one word, and others skip the word. Never use cursed text for
      information a reader needs.
    </p>

    <h2>Cleaning it up</h2>
    <p>
      To remove the marks in a text editor with regex support, delete all characters in the Unicode
      ranges U+0300–U+036F, U+1AB0–U+1AFF and U+20D0–U+20F0, and the base letters remain. In JavaScript,
      <code>text.normalize('NFD').replace(/\p&#123;M&#125;/gu, '')</code> does it in one line. It also
      removes real accents, so use it only on cursed text.
    </p>

    <h2>Generated in your browser</h2>
    <p>
      The cursed text generator stacks the marks with string operations as you type. Nothing is
      uploaded, no request is made, and the page works with the network disconnected.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
